\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{corl_2026} % Submission version: anonymous, line numbers enabled.
% \usepackage[final]{corl_2026} % Uncomment for camera-ready.

\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{subcaption}

\title{Why Token Pruning Crashes Some Robot Policies but Not Others:\\Vision Connectors and VLA Token Compressibility}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}

\keywords{vision-language-action models, token pruning, robot learning}

\input{introduction}

\input{related_work}

\input{method}

\input{experiments}

\input{analysis}

\input{conclusion}

\bibliography{references}

\input{appendix}

\end{document}

\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models encode observations as hundreds of vision tokens, creating a computational bottleneck for real-time robotic control. Token pruning offers a training-free remedy, yet its effect varies dramatically across architectures: at retention ratio $k{=}0.95$, $\pi_{0.5}$ preserves 98\% task success while SmolVLA collapses to 0\%---why?

We identify the vision-language connector as the primary architectural factor governing pruning tolerance. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms, computable in a single forward pass, predicts compressibility across the full architecture spectrum: from CV$\,{=}\,$0.038 (near-immune linear projection) to CV$\,{=}\,$0.314 (catastrophic pixel-shuffle collapse). Across five VLA architectures (450\,M--7\,B parameters) and four LIBERO manipulation suites, token-independent connectors degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors exhibit abrupt cliffs: SmolVLA collapses when just 6 of 128 tokens are removed.

Importance-based selection is not universally beneficial---it can be actively harmful. At moderate compression, L2 norm selection preserves 98\% success on $\pi_{0.5}$ where random yields 5\% (93\,pp gap); at aggressive ratios, random outperforms L2 by up to 36\,pp. Attention-based scoring exhibits within-model reversals across task suites. No scoring method is universally safe: task complexity amplifies connector-dependent patterns, with multi-step tasks magnifying entangling-connector losses by up to $20{\times}$. A zero-mask ablation isolates the mechanism: on $\pi_{0.5}$, zeroing 10\% of tokens preserves 98.0\% SR while physical removal collapses to 9.0\%---the 89\,pp gap is entirely attributable to position disruption, not information loss. For architectures where all deletion strategies fail, a lightweight re-encoding module (${\sim}$5\% extra parameters) recovers $>$60\% success at 75\% compression.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_hero_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview of VLA token compressibility.
\textbf{(a)}~The same robot observation produces qualitatively different token importance patterns through different vision connectors, quantified by the coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token norms.
\textbf{(b)}~Connector architecture determines pruning tolerance: independent connectors degrade gracefully, entangling connectors exhibit cliffs; task complexity amplifies the divergence.
\textbf{(c)}~A single forward pass CV diagnostic routes each architecture to an appropriate compression strategy: uniform pruning, importance-based scoring, or learned re-encoding.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure*}

Remove 5\% of vision tokens from SmolVLA and its success rate collapses from 59.2\% to near-zero (${\leq}$6.6\%) across all task suites and pruning methods. Apply the same 5\% reduction to $\pi_{0.5}$ and it retains 98.2\% under L2 norm selection. Both are Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on a Vision Transformer (ViT) encoder feeding tokens through a connector into a language model backbone; the critical difference is not model scale (450M vs.\ 4B parameters) but the connector architecture. Token pruning is well-established for ViTs~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit,bolya2023tome,liang2022evit} and a growing body of work extends it to VLAs~\cite{cheng2026vlaiap,xu2025vlacache,yang2025efficientvla,liu2025vlapruner}, but each method validates on a single architecture.

These methods implicitly assume that pruning transfers across VLA designs. Yet current VLAs employ fundamentally different vision connectors: linear projections~\cite{black2024pi0}, MLP projectors~\cite{kim2024openvla}, pixel-shuffle folding~\cite{shukor2025smolvla}, and learned encoders with cross-token self-attention~\cite{zheng2025xvla}. Each imposes distinct constraints on how visual information distributes across tokens. Deploying a pruning method validated on one connector type to another risks silent degradation or complete failure. No prior work evaluates pruning robustness across both connector architectures and task complexity.

We present an empirical study of VLA token compressibility, identifying the vision-language connector as the primary determinant of pruning robustness (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors (linear projections, MLP projectors) degrade gracefully, while token-entangling connectors produce qualitatively different failures: SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle collapses to near-zero (${\leq}$6.6\%) at every retention ratio, while X-VLA's BART encoder exhibits sharp threshold drops. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms captures this on a continuous spectrum (0.038 to 0.314 across all three connector families) and correlates with both pruning tolerance and method sensitivity: $\pi_{0.5}$ exhibits a $17{\times}$ greater method gap than X-VLA at $k{=}0.95$. Under sufficient compression, scoring effectiveness becomes connector-dependent: L2 norm aids $\pi_{0.5}$ across the cliff zone ($+$44.6\,pp on Long at $k{=}0.925$; $+$35.2\,pp on Goal at $k{=}0.85$, decaying to $+$5.6\,pp at $k{=}0.95$) but reverses at aggressive ratios ($-$36\,pp at $k{=}0.5$); on X-VLA it is anti-informative across all ratios ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp), while attention recovers near-baseline performance ($+$31\,pp on Long). Task complexity amplifies these connector-dependent patterns: under random pruning at $k{=}0.9$, X-VLA loses just 1.4 percentage points on single-step Object but 27.8 percentage points on multi-step Goal, a $20{\times}$ amplification.

For pixel-shuffle connectors where all pruning strategies yield near-zero SR, we introduce Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), a lightweight cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) that recovers over 60\% success rate at 75\% compression through learned re-encoding.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A compressibility analysis across five VLA architectures (450M to 7B) on four LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} task suites, identifying connector architecture as the primary determinant of pruning robustness, with task complexity and scoring method as modulating factors.
    \item CV of token L2 norms as a single-pass diagnostic that provides a coarse indicator of connector compressibility type (uniform, graded, or catastrophic) without downstream evaluation. Validated on four closed-loop architectures; we expect the trend to generalize but acknowledge the limited sample.
    \item A zero-mask ablation isolating the failure mechanism: on $\pi_{0.5}$, zeroing 10\% of tokens preserves 98.0\% SR while physical removal collapses to 9.0\% (89\,pp gap), proving position disruption---not information loss---drives the failure. Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) further confirms this by recovering a pixel-shuffle VLA from 0.0\% to over 60\% at 75\% compression through learned re-encoding.
\end{itemize}

