\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Models and Connectors}
\label{sec:models}

We evaluate token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families (Table~\ref{tab:model_properties}; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). These connectors differ in whether they process ViT patches independently or create cross-token dependencies, a distinction we formalize as \emph{coupling}.

\textbf{Linear projection} applies a single matrix multiplication per patch. $\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0} projects 256 patches through a linear layer; $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} uses a $\times\!\sqrt{d}$-scaled linear projection across 768 tokens from three cameras (CV$\,{<}\,$0.06).

\textbf{MLP projector} maps each patch independently through a multi-layer perceptron. OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} uses a three-layer MLP (256 tokens; CV$\,{=}\,$0.215); OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft}, an OpenVLA fine-tuning variant evaluated offline, shares the same connector (512 tokens; CV$\,{=}\,$0.219).

\textbf{Token-entangling connectors} introduce cross-token dependencies. X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} feeds linearly projected tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} (50 tokens). SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} applies pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding, concatenating 16 contiguous patches into each output token's channel dimension (128 tokens).

We classify coupling as \emph{independent} when each output token depends on a single input patch, and \emph{entangling} when output tokens encode information from multiple patches. Four models ($\pi_{0.5}$, X-VLA, OpenVLA, SmolVLA) are evaluated in closed-loop control on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}; $\pi_{0}$ and OFT are analyzed via offline metrics only.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture_v2.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector architectures across four closed-loop VLAs, ordered by increasing compressibility fragility. Token-independent connectors ($\pi_{0.5}$: linear projection; OpenVLA: 3-layer MLP) process each patch individually, yielding low CV. Token-entangling connectors (X-VLA: 12-layer BART encoder; SmolVLA: pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$) create cross-token dependencies and higher CV, predicting threshold-graded or catastrophic pruning failure.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{VLA models evaluated in this study. Coupling indicates whether the connector processes patches independently or creates cross-token dependencies. $N$ = number of vision tokens input to the LLM backbone. Models are grouped by evaluation protocol and ordered by CV within each group.}
\label{tab:model_properties}
\begin{tabular}{lrlcrll}
\toprule
Model & Params & Connector & $N$ & CV & Coupling & Eval \\
\midrule
$\pi_{0.5}$ & 4B & Linear ($\times\!\sqrt{d}$) & 768 & 0.055 & Indep.\ & Closed-loop \\
X-VLA & 880M & Linear + BART & 50 & 0.151 & Entangling & Closed-loop \\
OpenVLA & 7B & 3-layer MLP & 256 & 0.215 & Indep.\ & Closed-loop \\
SmolVLA & 450M & Pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ & 128 & 0.314 & Entangling & Closed-loop \\
\midrule
$\pi_{0}$ & 3.3B & Linear & 256 & 0.038 & Indep.\ & Offline \\
OFT$^\dagger$ & 7B & 3-layer MLP & 512 & 0.219 & Indep.\ & Offline \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$ OpenVLA fine-tuning variant, included for CV calibration (offline metrics only).}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:pruning_protocol}

We apply post-connector token pruning at retention ratio $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, where $k$ denotes the fraction of $N$ vision tokens retained. Given $N$ post-connector tokens, pruning produces $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens that are passed to the LLM backbone.

Two strategies bracket expected performance:

\textbf{L2 norm selection} ranks tokens by their L2 norm and retains the top-$k$ fraction. This importance-based strategy provides a favorable test: if a genuine importance hierarchy exists among tokens, L2 selection should outperform random baselines.

\textbf{Random subsampling} selects $\lfloor k \cdot N \rfloor$ tokens uniformly at random. Because it ignores token content entirely, random selection provides a conservative lower bound.

Both strategies physically remove unselected tokens and re-index position IDs to close gaps in the sequence. Pruned tokens are discarded, not replaced with zero vectors or mask tokens.

We prune post-connector because this point isolates the connector's effect on token information structure. Pre-connector pruning would test ViT redundancy; KV-cache pruning~\cite{xu2025vlacache} would test LLM attention patterns.

\subsection{Compressibility Diagnostic: Importance CV}
\label{sec:cv_diagnostic}

We quantify the heterogeneity of post-connector token representations using the coefficient of variation (CV) of their L2 norms:
\begin{equation}
\text{CV} = \frac{\sigma\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}{\mu\!\left(\{\|t_i\|_2\}_{i=1}^{N}\right)}
\label{eq:cv}
\end{equation}
where $t_i$ denotes the $i$-th post-connector vision token, and $\sigma$, $\mu$ are the standard deviation and mean computed over all $N$ token norms from a single observation.

CV is a single-pass diagnostic requiring one forward pass through the vision encoder and connector, with no downstream evaluation. The metric captures a continuous spectrum: $\pi_{0}$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.038) produces near-uniform norms, SmolVLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.314) exhibits high heterogeneity from pixel-shuffle, and the remaining models span the interval (Table~\ref{tab:model_properties}). CV correlates with both pruning tolerance and sensitivity to method choice: low-CV models have near-uniform tokens where \emph{which} tokens are removed matters more than \emph{how many}; high-CV entangling connectors fail catastrophically under any removal strategy. We report success rate (SR) as the primary closed-loop metric throughout.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:vtr}

Hard pruning fails for token-entangling connectors because removing any token destroys spatially distributed information that cannot be recovered from the remaining tokens. Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) addresses this through learned re-encoding: a cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) uses $M$ learned queries to attend over all $N$ source tokens, aggregating information from the entire spatial field into $M$ compressed representations. The compression ratio is controlled by $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$; $M{=}N{=}128$ serves as a passthrough control. A parameter-matched MLP adapter (average pooling + two-layer MLP, no cross-attention) isolates whether gains stem from cross-attention or from re-encoding itself.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

We evaluate vision token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three connector families on the LIBERO benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero}. LIBERO provides four task suites of increasing complexity: Object (object manipulation), Spatial (spatial reasoning), Goal (goal-conditioned tasks), and Long (long-horizon multi-step tasks). Each suite contains 10 tasks evaluated over 50 episodes, yielding 500 episodes per condition.

Four models are evaluated in closed-loop robotic control: $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B parameters, linear projection with $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling, 768 tokens from three cameras, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055), X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}, 50 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151), OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, three-layer MLP projector, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215), and SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M, pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding, 128 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314). Two additional models are analyzed via offline metrics only: $\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, linear projection, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.038) and OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} (7B, three-layer MLP, 512 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.219). All six architectures contribute to the CV diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:cv_sensitivity}). Table~\ref{tab:model_properties} summarizes model properties and connector details.

We follow the pruning protocol of Section~\ref{sec:pruning_protocol} at retention ratios $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, reporting mean success rate (SR) over 500 episodes with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{Two-Axis Framework Overview}
\label{sec:two_axis}

The pruning response depends on token independence imposed by the connector and task complexity (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; token-entangling connectors exhibit sharp, task-dependent collapse.

