\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Re-encoding and CV diagnostics.}
Both cross-attention and MLP modules achieve comparable recovery ($p = 0.15$); the stable M-curve ($M{=}16 \approx M{=}128$) confirms token count is secondary once entanglement is broken (Appendix~\ref{app:reencoding_analysis}). CV captures effective pipeline complexity beyond connector labels, differentiating X-VLA's threshold-graded behavior (CV$\,{=}\,$0.151) from $\pi_0$'s uniformly critical profile (CV$\,{=}\,$0.038); the interaction reverses at $k{=}0.3$ (Appendices~\ref{app:cv_strategy_interaction},~\ref{app:cv_extended}). For extended mechanistic analysis of how connector architecture creates the observed token independence structure, see Appendices~\ref{app:connector_analysis} and~\ref{app:reencoding_analysis}.

\paragraph{Task complexity compresses the importance margin.}
The CV diagnostic correctly identifies connector-level compressibility, but practitioners must additionally consider task complexity. On LIBERO-Object (single-step manipulation), L2 norm selection outperforms random on OpenVLA at $k{=}0.9$ ($+$7.8\,pp: L2 70.6\% vs.\ Random 62.8\%). However, this advantage is not robust to deeper compression: at $k{=}0.85$, the importance margin flips to $-$5.2\,pp (Random 60.4\% $>$ L2 55.2\% $\approx$ Attention 55.0\%). The three methods exhibit distinct cliff locations: L2 cliffs earliest ($k{=}0.9{\to}0.85$, $-$15.4\,pp), random next ($k{=}0.85{\to}0.7$, $-$16\,pp), and attention latest ($k{=}0.75{\to}0.7$, $-$9.4\,pp), with attention maintaining a plateau from $k{=}0.85$ to $k{=}0.75$ ($-$1.8\,pp). This staggered cliff onset defines an effective compression window for each scoring method (Figure~\ref{fig:openvla_sweep}). At $k{=}0.5$, all three methods converge within 4.8\,pp (L2: 39.2\%, Random: 35.8\%, Attention: 40.6\%), and scoring choice ceases to matter. On LIBERO-Goal, degradation is smooth with no cliff: all three methods converge within 2\,pp at $k{=}0.7$ (L2: 68.4\%, Random: 68.4\%, Attention: 66.4\%). Notably, random pruning at $k{=}0.9$ \emph{exceeds} the unpruned baseline (79.6\% vs.\ 74.8\%, $+$4.8\,pp; cross-seed confirmed at 80.4\%), suggesting that OpenVLA's 256 vision tokens are over-provisioned for goal-conditioned tasks---random removal acts as implicit regularization, reducing redundant token interference. On LIBERO-Long (multi-step, temporally extended tasks), the advantage vanishes earlier and inverts more sharply. At $k{=}0.9$, all three methods converge within 3.2\,pp (Random: 46.8\%, Attention: 44.0\%, L2: 43.6\%). At $k{=}0.85$, attention-based scoring \emph{increases} to 45.8\% while L2 drops to 38.8\%, opening a 7.0\,pp gap. At $k{=}0.7$, the method ranking fully inverts relative to Object: Random (38.8\%) $>$ L2 (31.2\%) $>$ Attention (24.0\%). The negative importance margin amplifies with compression: the Attention--Random gap widens from $-$2.8\,pp ($k{=}0.9$) to $-$14.8\,pp ($k{=}0.7$), with attention degrading $2.5{\times}$ faster than random. Multi-step tasks distribute critical visual information across tokens with heterogeneous norm profiles; both L2 and attention scoring cluster on locally salient tokens, sacrificing the spatial diversity that compositional reasoning requires. Random selection, by preserving uniform spatial coverage, provides a more robust signal under these conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:openvla_sweep}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_openvla_sweep.pdf}
\caption{OpenVLA scoring method comparison across four LIBERO suites. On Object, L2 leads at $k{=}0.9$ ($+$7.8\,pp) but the margin flips at $k{=}0.85$ ($-$5.2\,pp, Random wins). Goal degrades smoothly with three-method convergence at $k{=}0.7$. On Long, the ranking inverts with compression. No single scoring method dominates across suites or retention ratios.}
\label{fig:openvla_sweep}
\end{figure}

This is a concrete manifestation of the two-axis framework (Section~\ref{sec:two_axis}): Axis~1 (connector architecture) determines \emph{whether} pruning is viable; Axis~2 (task complexity) determines \emph{which} scoring method succeeds. CV remains a valid connector diagnostic, but the optimal scoring strategy requires knowledge of both axes.

\paragraph{Position encoding mediates the V-shape recovery.}
The monotonic L2 curve versus the non-monotonic random curve on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}) reflects an interaction between token selection strategy and positional encoding. L2 norm scoring preserves spatial locality: retained tokens form near-contiguous clusters in the ViT grid, producing continuous position shifts after re-indexing that Gemma's RoPE accommodates smoothly---hence monotonic degradation (86.8\% $\to$ 50.2\% from $k{=}0.95$ to $k{=}0.5$). Random selection creates scattered gaps that produce position aliasing after re-indexing. At $k{\approx}0.9$, the aliasing is maximally destructive: a small number of randomly placed gaps create highly irregular inter-token spacing, collapsing SR to 31.8\%. Per-task analysis reveals this aggregate is driven by specific tasks with position-sensitive spatial reasoning: Task~4 (``put mugs on plates'') drops from 80\% at $k{=}0.7$ to 12\% at $k{=}0.85$, contributing disproportionately to the V-shape minimum. At $k{=}0.7$, enough tokens are removed that the surviving positions become approximately uniform again, and SR recovers to 64.6\%---exceeding the $k{=}0.9$ level by $2{\times}$. The zero-mask ablation (Section~\ref{sec:zeromask}) provides causal confirmation: zeroing token content while preserving positions yields 98.0\% SR, while physically removing the same tokens collapses to 9.0\%. Position encoding, not information content, is the binding constraint---and scoring method determines whether that constraint is violated.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All experiments use LIBERO simulation; models differ in backbone and fusion, preventing causal isolation (Appendix~\ref{app:confounders}). $\pi_0$ is offline only; the CV correlation spans three models; the cliff model captures onset sharpness (RMSE$\,{=}\,$0.025). The position aliasing explanation for the V-shape recovery (above) is consistent with our data but remains a hypothesis---direct measurement of RoPE frequency interference under non-contiguous indexing is needed for confirmation. At extreme compression ($k{=}0.7$), random and L2 converge within noise on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long (64.6\% vs.\ 63.2\%, $\Delta{=}1.4$\,pp $<$ cross-seed variation), consistent with the broader pattern that scoring methods lose discriminative power under heavy compression.

